  ByT5: 4,200 sentences from byt5_preds_segmentation-lemma-morphosyntax_sighum_test.jsonl, scored against the SAME external reference
  and the same score() as cuSHR -- this column IS a head-to-head.
  M levels are n/a: ByT5 emits full UD feature bundles
  (Case=Nom|Gender=Masc|Number=Sing) while the reference is DCS cng integers
  (29, -153). Scoring them directly would read as total tagging failure rather
  than an unmapped vocabulary. NOTE the observed output is NOT the compressed
  SNM codes of the paper's figures, so sanskrit_tags.tsv is not needed to bridge it.

  ORACLE = our own gold path scored against the same external reference, over the 4,196 sentences that have one.
  It is a CEILING, and it is not 100: DCS lemmatises participles to the verbal
  root where SHR gives the participial stem, so L / L+M / S+L+M measure
  convention agreement as much as model quality. Read every cuSHR number
  against its oracle, not against ByT5.
  (tol) = lemma matched through ingest.lemma_candidates; upper bound.
  'ByT5 paper' is a DIFFERENT CORPUS (DCS April-2024, 8,398 test), shown for
  category alignment only. It is NOT the --pred-jsonl column, which is measured
  here on these 4,200 sentences.

  CAVEAT on the ByT5 S column: the released chronbmm/sanskrit5-multitask
  is trained on DCS compound conventions, while this reference uses SIGHUM's.
  Most of its segmentation errors are compound-boundary disagreements
  (droRaputraH vs droRa putraH, alOlyena vs a lOlyena), not failures to
  segment. Their published 93.83 on SIGHUM comes from a model fine-tuned ON
  SIGHUM; this is the off-the-shelf multitask checkpoint, so the S column
  understates it. L is unaffected -- both sides use DCS lemma conventions.

